Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• NOAA_DATA_PLUS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES  
  – "zip_code_geom": geometry of each ZIP code polygon – will be used with ST_WITHIN.  
  – "zip_code": postal code that will be displayed.  
  – "city": city name that will be displayed.  
  – "state_code": two–letter state that will be displayed.  

• NOAA_DATA_PLUS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES  
  – "latitude", "longitude": location of the storm centre at each observation – turned into a point.  
  – "name": storm name – filter out NULL / empty to exclude unnamed storms.  

Conditions that will be useful  
1. ST_WITHIN( ST_POINT("longitude","latitude"), "zip_code_geom" )  
2. "name" IS NOT NULL AND TRIM("name") <> ''  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake queries (all limited to 20 rows where practical) progressing from very simple inspection to a final‑form query that fulfils the requested result.  
All identifiers are double‑quoted, and every query is annotated.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Quick look at hurricane rows to understand the "name", "latitude", "longitude" columns.
SELECT "sid", "iso_time", "name", "latitude", "longitude"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Show how many hurricane rows have no name (NULL or empty string).
SELECT
    COUNT(*) AS "total_rows",
    COUNT_IF("name" IS NULL OR TRIM("name") = '') AS "unnamed_rows"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES";
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Distinct list of non‑empty hurricane names (alphabetical) for sampling.
SELECT DISTINCT "name"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES"
WHERE "name" IS NOT NULL AND TRIM("name") <> ''
ORDER BY "name"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Peek at ZIP code polygons and basic descriptive columns.
SELECT "zip_code", "city", "state_code",
       ST_ASTEXT("zip_code_geom") AS "wkt_sample"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Verify a random ST_WITHIN result (join first 20 hurricanes to zip codes).
SELECT h."sid", h."name", z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES"  h
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES" z
    ON ST_WITHIN( ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude"), z."zip_code_geom")
WHERE h."name" IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(h."name") <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Count of hurricanes falling into each ZIP (without names filter, quick sample).
SELECT z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code",
       COUNT(*) AS "hurricane_obs_in_zip"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES" h
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES" z
    ON ST_WITHIN(ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude"), z."zip_code_geom")
GROUP BY z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code"
ORDER BY "hurricane_obs_in_zip" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Same as #6 but ignore unnamed observations.
SELECT z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code",
       COUNT(*) AS "named_hurricane_obs_in_zip"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES" h
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES" z
  ON ST_WITHIN(ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude"), z."zip_code_geom")
WHERE h."name" IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(h."name") <> ''
GROUP BY z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code"
ORDER BY "named_hurricane_obs_in_zip" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Aggregate unique hurricane names per ZIP (sample only).
SELECT z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code",
       ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT h."name") AS "storm_names_array"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES" h
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES" z
  ON ST_WITHIN(ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude"), z."zip_code_geom")
WHERE h."name" IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(h."name") <> ''
GROUP BY z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Convert the distinct names array into a sorted, comma‑separated string (per ZIP).
SELECT
    z."zip_code",
    z."city",
    z."state_code",
    ARRAY_SIZE( ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT h."name") ) AS "hurricane_count",
    ARRAY_TO_STRING( ARRAY_SORT( ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT h."name") ), ', ' ) AS "hurricane_list"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES" h
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES" z
  ON ST_WITHIN(ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude"), z."zip_code_geom")
WHERE h."name" IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(h."name") <> ''
GROUP BY z."zip_code", z."city", z."state_code"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final complete query – count of distinct hurricane names and their alphabetically‑sorted list for each City / ZIP / State, ordered by count descending.
SELECT
    z."city"          AS "city",
    z."zip_code"      AS "zip_code",
    z."state_code"    AS "state",
    ARRAY_SIZE( ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT h."name") )                    AS "hurricane_count",
    ARRAY_TO_STRING( ARRAY_SORT( ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT h."name") ), ', ' ) AS "hurricane_names"
FROM "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."NOAA_HURRICANES"."HURRICANES" h
JOIN "NOAA_DATA_PLUS"."GEO_US_BOUNDARIES"."ZIP_CODES" z
  ON ST_WITHIN(ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude"), z."zip_code_geom")
WHERE h."name" IS NOT NULL
  AND TRIM(h."name") <> ''
GROUP BY
    z."city",
    z."zip_code",
    z."state_code"
ORDER BY "hurricane_count" DESC;
```

Each query moves one step closer to the analytical goal while helping you inspect data, validate joins, and confirm logic before running the final aggregation.
